              <p> 
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="3"> 
                <b>PMWATCH</b> -- March 12, 2002 -- Those who have accused Israel 
                of imitating the practices of their erstwhile tormentors, the 
                Nazis, have always been sneered off as polemicists prone to hyperbole 
                and exaggeration. Below is a report from Serge Schmemann of the 
                New York Times pointing out that Palestinian "detainees had identity 
                numbers written on their arms in ink" -- a practice notoriously 
                evoking the concentration camps of Auschwitz. Since the report, 
                Haaretz has confirmed the allegation (see Ha'aretz ticker updates 
                at 11:28 am Tuesday, March 12) and has reported that Chief of 
                Staff Lt.-Gen. Shaul Mofaz has ordered a stop to this practice. 
                </font></p>
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="3">Is 
                there a need to say more? Do we need crematories before an outrage 
                is sounded? No wonder millions of Jews died before the Americans 
                and the rest of the world spoke up <i>after</i> the Holocaust. 
                And since then, they have been expiating their sin of silence. 
                Must history repeat itself?</font> </p>
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="3">Palestine 
                Media Watch -- <a href="/sites/pmwatch.org/index.html" class="dead" aria-disabled="true">http://www.pmwatch.org</a> 
                -- (610) 993 - 0608</font></p>
            </td>
