                        <p align="left"><font face="Arial"><b>Your letter</b></font> 
                          <textarea name="txtComments" cols="60" rows="30">http://www.nytimes.com/2002/03/12/international/middleeast/12MIDE.html
March 12, 2002

The New York Times

At Least 17 Are Killed in Israeli Raid at Palestinian Camp in Gaza

By SERGE SCHMEMANN

JERUSALEM, Tuesday, March 12 — The Israeli sweep through Palestinian refugee camps reached 

into the teeming Jabaliya camp in Gaza overnight, and Palestinians said at least 17 

residents were killed and scores were injured trying to block the tanks and armored 

personnel carriers in the predawn darkness. 

Tanks also rumbled into Ramallah, where residents said sounds of battle were heard from the 

Amari refugee camp.

The latest incursions followed a day in which refugee camps in Bethlehem and Qalqilya were 

raided and searched. The operation, which began March 1 and has included dragnets through 

camps in Jenin, Nablus and the Gaza Strip, is by far the largest Israeli military operation 

against the Palestinians since the Oslo agreements of 1993 gave the Palestinians control 

over some of their lands.

The escalation of the raids came even as anticipation grew of a new American push for a 

cease-fire. In a gesture intended to demonstrate his readiness to cooperate in the upcoming 

mission of Gen. Anthony C. Zinni, the American envoy to the Middle East, Prime Minister 

Ariel Sharon announced on Monday that the Palestinian leader, Yasir Arafat, was free to 

resume traveling about the West Bank and Gaza, although not abroad. 

Mr. Sharon has repeatedly vowed to continue the military campaign against the Palestinian 

Authority, following his declaration last week that only a heavy death toll would bring the 

Palestinians to the table. The primary target has been the refugee camps, which were set up 

in 1948 for people displaced by the Arab-Israeli wars and have swelled into crowded slums 

seething with resentment against Israel. 

There are 19 such camps in the West Bank, eight in the Gaza Strip and dozens in Lebanon, 

Syria and Jordan, where the population has swelled to 3.8 million, according to the United 

Nations Relief and Works Agency, which is charged with providing services to the refugees.

Of all of these, Jabaliya, north of Gaza City, is the largest, and it is there that Hamas, 

the militant Islamic movement, has its strongest base. Details of the latest raids were not 

yet available. But the pattern in earlier operations has been for Israeli troops in full 

battle gear, backed by tanks, armored personnel carriers and Apache attack helicopters, to 

sweep through the narrow streets, rounding up hundreds of men for questioning and smashing 

into houses to conduct searches for weapons and bomb workshops.

Dr. Muawia Hassami, director of emergency services at the Ashifa Hospital in Gaza City, 

reported 17 dead and 45 injured as of 2 a.m. "We are working to full capacity in 12 

operating rooms," he said.

Israeli gunfire killed five Palestinians in the West Bank and Gaza during the day Monday, 

and a Palestinian security officer was killed by Israeli fire in southern Gaza on Monday 

night at the same time the fighting was taking place at Jebaliya, The Associated Press 

reported.

In the Dheisheh camp on the edge of Bethlehem, and in Qalqilya on Monday, Israeli troops 

ordered all males ages 15 to 45 to present themselves. In Bethlehem, several hundred men 

were assembled in a stone- cutting factory; in Qalqilya at a central school. There, 

plainclothes intelligence officers questioned them, releasing some immediately and taking 

others, often handcuffed and blindfolded, to military camps. 

The army said it had no figures on the detentions, though radio reports said more than 1,000 

men were rounded up. In the only sweep for which figures have been made available, in 

Tulkarm last week, 66 men remain in detention of about 500 rounded up. Palestinians said 

that in anticipation of the latest raids, most of the men wanted by Israel had left the 

camps. 

Following reports that some of the detainees had identity numbers written on their arms in 

ink, Mr. Arafat accused Israeli troops of "new Nazi racism." In an interview with Abu Dhabi 

television, portions of which were shown on Israeli television, Mr. Arafat said, referring 

to the identity numbers: "Is that not what they say the Nazis did to the Jews? What do they 

have to say about this matter?"

An Israeli Army spokeswoman said, "This is an obscene and absurd statement which hardly 

warrants further response."

The announcement that Mr. Arafat was free to travel in his territories drew ire from 

Palestinian officials. 

"It's ridiculous and disgusting," said Yasir Abed Rabbo, the Palestinians minister of 

information. "Technically, there is no place to land his helicopter — if he had any 

helicopter left. Politically, it's just another game they are playing. They want to contain 

the American proposals and ideals." 

Mr. Sharon, he said, "is playing with fire and blood. He is not serious."

The open question is whether Israel will allow Mr. Arafat to attend an Arab League summit 

meeting in Beirut on March 27. There, a Saudi proposal to promise Israel full normalization 

of relations with Arab countries, in exchange for a withdrawal from territories it occupied, 

will be brought before all 22 Arab leaders.

Last week, Mr. Sharon lifted one major obstacle to a formal cease-fire when he agreed to 

abandon his demand for seven days of calm before entering into negotiations on the American 

plan for a halt to hostilities. American and Israeli officials in Washington acknowledged 

privately that they had put strong pressure on Mr. Sharon to do so. But the United States 

appeared less interested in Mr. Arafat's mobility. 

"We think the Israelis should take a careful look at this question of Mr. Arafat's travel 

and determine what's in their interests," a State Department spokesman said.

Despite the continued military operations in the Palestinian territories and constant 

threats of Palestinian terror attacks, both sides looked forward anxiously to the arrival of 

General Zinni, a retired marine.

A suggestion by Secretary of State Colin L. Powell that General Zinni would be coming with 

monitors aroused curiosity, since the Israelis have generally opposed the idea of 

international observers to monitor a cease-fire. A Foreign Ministry official said he thought 

the reference was to assistants who would join General Zinni in keeping track of 

developments, not formal monitors.

Right-wing Israelis packed into Rabin Square in Tel Aviv on Monday evening to demonstrate in 

favor of more decisive military action against the Palestinians. Crowd estimates varied from 

30,000 to double that.

Demonstrators carried banners saying "Get the Enemies Out of the Country" and "Sharon, learn 

to fight like George Bush — Bomb them!" and other messages urging Mr. Sharon to topple the 

Palestinian Authority. The speakers included retired Brig. Gen. Effir Eitam, who appealed to 

Mr. Sharon, "From here we embrace you, we encourage you, we call you to rise and be the Lion 

of Judah, and the people will follow."

Security was extraordinary, with 1,600 uniformed police officers and soldiers checking 

everyone who entered the square.</textarea>
